<body>
<nav class="guide-nav">
  <a href="/research/">← Research</a>
  <span class="logo">Seascape Vacations</span>
</nav>

<main class="page">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <span class="eyebrow">Owner Fee + Revenue Leak Benchmark</span>
      <span class="scope-pill">5-property Gulf Coast scope</span>
      <h1>Your management fee is not the whole revenue leak.</h1>
      <p class="hero-lead">Most owners compare 15%, 20%, and 25% management fees. That misses the bigger stack: OTA drag, direct-booking mix, pricing discipline, and how clearly your manager explains what is happening to owner net.</p>
      <p class="hero-support">Built from Seascape's current 5-home Gulf Coast portfolio, with scenario math labeled separately from observed operating data.</p>
      <div class="proof-rail">
        {% for label in proofLabels %}
        <span class="proof-chip">{{ label }}</span>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-side">
      <div class="side-panel">
        <span class="eyebrow">Observed Seascape portfolio data</span>
        <div class="stats-grid">
          {% for stat in benchmark.stats %}
          <div class="stat-card">
            <strong>{{ stat.value }}</strong>
            <span>{{ stat.label }}</span>
            <p>{{ stat.detail }}</p>
          </div>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="side-panel">
        <span class="eyebrow">How To Read This</span>
        <ul class="quick-list">
          <li>The benchmark uses Seascape's 5-home Gulf Coast operating data where observed.</li>
          <li>Scenario math is labeled separately from observed operating data.</li>
          <li>It frames the owner-net conversation before a property-specific teardown.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="source-note">
    <p><strong>Source note:</strong> {{ benchmark.sourceLabel }}. This benchmark is a decision aid, not a revenue forecast or market-wide fee survey. Scenario math is labeled separately from observed operating facts.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">The wrong comparison</span>
      <h2>A lower management fee can still leave owner net exposed.</h2>
      <p>The headline percentage matters, but it is not the whole economic picture. If most bookings arrive through high-drag channels, if direct mix is weak, if cleaning-heavy subtotals inflate OTA fees, or if rate decisions are not explained clearly, the owner can lose margin even when the management fee looks competitive.</p>
      <p><strong>The question is not only "What do they charge?" It is "What actually reaches my owner statement?"</strong></p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-grid">
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Headline fee</span>
        <h3>Management fee</h3>
        <p>The fee still matters, but it is only one piece of the owner-net equation. A lower-looking manager can still underperform if the calendar, channel mix, or operations are weak.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Observed Seascape portfolio data</span>
        <h3>OTA host drag</h3>
        <p>Airbnb's fee is not just a platform line item. The observed Seascape benchmark shows 13.4% host-fee drag on post-October 2025 bookings, including fees applied to the full subtotal.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Observed Seascape portfolio data</span>
        <h3>Direct payment cost</h3>
        <p>Direct processing at about 2.9% does not solve everything by itself. It does show how much owner margin can move when a booking is acquired without the heavier OTA layer.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="stack-card">
        <span class="stack-metric">Property-level review</span>
        <h3>Rate and operations discipline</h3>
        <p>Discount-heavy fills, weak premium-week protection, turnover misses, and poor owner communication rarely show up cleanly in a flat management-fee comparison. They still hit the same payout.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">Channel drag</span>
      <h2>The channel can move owner net as much as the fee conversation.</h2>
      <p>In Seascape's approved benchmark inputs, observed Airbnb host-fee drag averages 13.4% on post-Oct 2025 Seascape bookings. Direct payment cost is 2.9%. That gap is why channel mix belongs in the owner-net conversation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-panel">
        <span class="eyebrow">Observed Seascape portfolio data</span>
        <h3>Airbnb Drag vs Direct Payment Cost</h3>
        <div class="bar-list">
          <div class="bar-row">
            <div class="bar-label">Airbnb host drag</div>
            <div class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:89%;background:var(--gold)"></span></div>
            <div class="bar-value">13.4%</div>
          </div>
          <div class="bar-row">
            <div class="bar-label">Direct payment cost</div>
            <div class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:19%;background:var(--brand-dark)"></span></div>
            <div class="bar-value">2.9%</div>
          </div>
          <div class="bar-row">
            <div class="bar-label">Gap to audit</div>
            <div class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:70%;background:var(--brand)"></span></div>
            <div class="bar-value">10.5 pts</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="mini-note">Observed Seascape channel-cost references. Airbnb drag is an observed Seascape average; direct cost is a processing reference. This chart is not a market-wide fee survey.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-panel">
        <span class="eyebrow">Where Owner Revenue Actually Leaks</span>
        <ul class="quick-list">
          <li>Airbnb takes its cut on the full subtotal, including cleaning-heavy stays.</li>
          <li>A booking can look fine on occupancy while still leaking margin through the channel mix.</li>
          <li>The owner question is not whether direct fixes everything. It is whether the current mix is more expensive than it needs to be.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">Observed Seascape examples</span>
      <h2>Three examples from the current benchmark inputs.</h2>
      <p>These examples are narrow on purpose. They show what Seascape has observed in its own operating data, not what every Gulf Coast home should expect.</p>
    </div>
